+++ b/functions/src/services/fhirExchange/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,164 @@
+<!--
+SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Stanford University and the project authors (see CONTRIBUTORS.md)
+SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
+-->
+
+# FHIR exchange receiver
+
+This directory contains the source-neutral storage boundary for mobile FHIR
+exchange. It does not depend on the repository's legacy partial FHIR models.
+
+`FhirBundleReceiver.receive` accepts an exact JSON string whose parsed object has
+`resourceType` `Bundle` and `type` `collection`. It does not establish FHIR R4
+base-resource or profile conformance; those remain independent producer and
+integration-CI gates. A complete `(system, value)` identifier names the source
+record. `sourceVersion` is the producer's journaled data revision;
+`eventSequence` is the producer/outbox sequence that orders this source
+record's exchange events.
+The source version is non-negative and the event sequence is positive; both are
+canonical decimal strings of at most 128 digits, so JavaScript cannot truncate
+them. Identifier systems are limited to 2,048 UTF-8 bytes and values to 4,096
+UTF-8 bytes. The receiver retains the submitted JSON bytes. It reads only the
+elements needed to index Observations and leaves Device, Provenance, profiles,
+extensions, and logical References untouched.
+
+Every Observation in the Bundle, including an entered-in-error tombstone, must
+carry that exact envelope source Identifier pair among its identifiers. Other
+identifier systems remain available for stable one-to-many output identity; a
+second value under the envelope source system is rejected. This rule prevents
+one envelope from mixing source records. The receiver matches the exact pair
+and never guesses a source identifier from identifier order.
+
+The runtime envelope has this exact shape:
+
+```json
+{
+  "operation": "upsert",
+  "sourceIdentifier": {
+    "system": "https://example.org/fhir/NamingSystem/source-record",
+    "value": "record-123"
+  },
+  "sourceVersion": "1776204600123456789",
+  "eventSequence": "42",
+  "bundleJson": "{\"resourceType\":\"Bundle\",\"type\":\"collection\",\"entry\":[{\"resource\":{\"resourceType\":\"Observation\",\"identifier\":[{\"system\":\"https://example.org/fhir/NamingSystem/source-record\",\"value\":\"record-123\"},{\"system\":\"https://example.org/fhir/NamingSystem/output\",\"value\":\"output-1\"}],\"status\":\"final\",\"code\":{\"text\":\"Example\"}}}]}"
+}
+```
+
+`operation` is exactly lowercase `upsert` or `delete`. `sourceVersion` records
+the producer's source revision evidence. `eventSequence` is the authoritative,
+positive producer/outbox ordering for events about that source record; a retry
+reuses the same sequence and exact bytes. A higher event sequence is accepted
+even when a filter change, reactivation, or resynchronization leaves
+`sourceVersion` equal or lower. A delete keeps the last source version, advances
+the event sequence, and sends its complete tombstone Bundle. Neither value is a
+JavaScript number. There is no caller-supplied checksum: the receiver derives a
+SHA-256 payload digest from the exact `bundleJson` string. Unsupported envelope
+fields are rejected instead of being silently ignored.
+
+The trusted account/study partition is constructor context, not an envelope
+field. Source and Observation-index document IDs hash canonical structured
+`(system, value)` pairs for both the partition and identifier under distinct
+domains. They never concatenate delimiter-sensitive or client-selected keys.
+
+Exact Bundle bytes and the derived identity manifest are immutable, pinned
+Cloud Storage object generations. Firestore stores only their digest-checked
+references and lifecycle metadata. Before either upload begins, the receiver
+transactionally registers both exact object paths, digests, and sizes against
+the expected source head. Each upload checks that registration before and after
+writing; if a staging lease has already been collected, the just-written pinned
+generation is removed. Expired staging records let the retention worker find
+and remove registered objects after a crash before the final commit. The final
+source transaction consumes the staging record atomically, so a collector and
+an accepting receiver cannot both own it.
+
+The default upload-staging lifetime is 24 hours. A deployment must keep the
+maximum intake execution time below its configured staging lifetime; this is
+comfortably above the Cloud Functions execution limit but remains an explicit
+requirement if another runtime calls the library. Complete
+Observation-identifier claims are staged separately in deterministic pages of
+200 claims. Each page has a receipt that is invalidated if any reservation is
+superseded. One final Firestore transaction checks every page receipt and
+atomically commits the source-head pointer and durable delivery record while
+deleting the upload-staging record and every page receipt. A claim becomes
+active only when its generation matches that source head. This supports series
+larger than one Firestore transaction without exposing a partial source
+revision. Interrupted reservations expire. The retention worker removes their
+page receipts and retires complete-identifier documents only after re-reading
+every candidate owner source and proving that no slot names a current
+generation. Current claims are normalized and deferred; stale claims from
+replacement or deletion generations are removed.
+
+Construction requires a positive, bounded terminal-history retention duration;
+there is no implicit forever-retention mode. Each accepted event owns unique
+generation-scoped Bundle and manifest objects. The leased retention worker
+deletes an expired terminal delivery only after a transaction proves it is not
+the current source generation. It retries partial object deletion safely and
+defers an expired current generation for a later check. Object-deletion
+failures use bounded exponential backoff; exhausting the retry policy moves the
+metadata to an explicit `garbage-collection-failed` quarantine for operator
+inspection instead of allowing one poison record to block later pages. The
+scheduled function drains successive indexed pages every 15 minutes within a
+bounded work and runtime budget. A deployment chooses the duration under its
+approved research, legal, and participant-deletion policy rather than
+inheriting a value invented by this library.
+
+Device, DeviceMetric, Patient, ResearchStudy, Provenance, and other companion
+entries are not split into a second resource store. They move with the exact
+Bundle as one source generation, so advancing the source head cannot leave an
+older companion entry attached to the current generation. Older immutable
+Bundles remain retained delivery/audit history rather than current resources.
+
+An identical event sequence and payload is a replay. Reusing an event sequence
+for other content is a conflict. Older events do not mutate storage. A deletion
+retains the last source version and provides a complete Bundle covering the active Observations with
+`status=entered-in-error`, and advances only `eventSequence`. A replacement
+must retain every active Observation or include its complete
+`entered-in-error` tombstone. Requiring producer-supplied tombstones keeps
+high-precision FHIR JSON lossless; the receiver never reconstructs a partial
+Observation. Delivery uses an at-least-once outbox. Exhausted retries remain
+stored with the `dead-letter` state.
+
+The worker transactionally leases a pending delivery before invoking its
+callback. A crashed attempt becomes eligible again after the bounded lease and
+cannot overwrite the state of a newer lease. Delivery and the Firestore
+acknowledgement still cannot form one transaction, so independent retries may
+arrive more than once or out of order. The downstream callback therefore
+applies `eventSequence` with the same per-`sourceKey` compare-and-set rule as
+intake, ignores lower sequences, treats an exact same-sequence delivery as a
+replay, and rejects a same-sequence content conflict. It is also idempotent by
+`deliveryId`; delivery-ID idempotence alone is not an ordering guarantee.
+`redriveDeadLetter` is the explicit operator/service entry point for a retained
+dead letter. It reuses the same delivery ID and exact object generation, resets
+the bounded attempt cycle, and records the redrive count.
+
+`firestore.indexes.json` supplies the pending-delivery, staging-retention,
+terminal-retention, identifier-retirement, and claim-page-retirement query
+indexes. Nested identity maps that are never queried are exempted from indexing
+to avoid needless fan-out.
+
+The receiver has operational safety bounds of 16 MiB per Bundle, 50,000
+Observations, and 80,000 distinct complete identifier tokens. These are server
+resource limits, not Health Connect semantics. The receiver rejects an entire
+oversized event; it never truncates a source output set. A producer must retain
+that event for retry or quarantine and must not advance its source token as if
+the event had been accepted.
+
+This service is not an authentication endpoint, and this proposal deliberately
+does not export an HTTPS/callable intake function or an outbox delivery
+scheduler. The repository has no authoritative mapping from a caller to the
+required account/study partition and no approved downstream FHIR sink. The one
+scheduled function added here only enforces retention for data already accepted
+by a future integration. Intake and delivery integration are deferred to step
+8. That integration must derive authorization and scope from trusted server
+context, wire an idempotent/order-aware delivery callback, schedule that
+delivery worker, and expose dead-letter redrive only to an authorized operator.
+It must not use a client-selected account partition. Step 8 must also extend
+the authorized participant/account-deletion workflow to remove that
+partition's source heads, claims, deliveries, staging records, and pinned
+Storage generations. Time-based history retention deliberately cannot infer
+that account-to-partition mapping, and it never deletes the current source head.
+
+The boundary performs only resource-shape and identity checks needed for safe
+storage; it does not establish FHIR R4 base-resource or profile conformance.
+Those remain a producer and integration-CI responsibility using the HL7 FHIR
+Validator.
